  "rationale": "The `Cargo.toml` metadata appears to declare the right behavior, but the `{impl}` marker is on the wrong table and no `{test}` references the requirement.\n\n**What the requirement needs.** The plugin must request a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI. It must also use that installation as the session-start hook command.\n\n**What `Cargo.toml` declares.**\n- `[[package.metadata.symposium.installations]]` (lines 24-30) has `source = \"cargo\"`, `crate = \"habeas\"`, `executable = \"habeas\"` and `global = true`.\n- `[[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]]` (lines 32-37) has `event = \"SessionStart\"` and `command = \"habeas\"`, with `args = [\"hook\", \"session-start\"]`.\n\n**Gap 1: the `{impl}` marker is on the wrong node.**\n- The only `{impl}` for `requirements.integration.symposium_cli` is the comment at line 23.\n- The outline attaches that comment to `table[package.metadata.symposium.skills]`, which holds only `source.path = \"assets/skills\"`.\n- That table says nothing about installing the CLI or about the hook command.\n- The installations table (lines 24-30) is actually marked `{impl}` for `symposium_setup_hook` (line 31), not for `symposium_cli`.\n- The hooks table, which sets the hook command, has no `{impl}` reference to this requirement.\n- So the code that does implement the requirement is not marked for it. The marked node implements nothing of it.\n\n**Gap 2: no `{test}` references.**\n- `references` returns only that one `{impl}` site. There are no `{test}` or `{cite}` sites.\n- `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py` does touch the declaration. It asserts that the cargo-installation block appears exactly once. It then swaps in a local runner so that the hook command resolves to a local executable.\n- It never checks that the installation is global and Cargo-sourced, or that the hook command is the installed `habeas`. Its `{test}` markers are for `symposium_setup_hook` and `symposium`, not this requirement.\n- Without a test marker, the criteria are not met. The requirement is not so simple or impractical to test that tests could be omitted.",
